{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZp5D3DC7tjn7eahfeE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral Co(II), **Dq = 433 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**, from the standard ligand-field formulas. I did not use any of the session's tools.\n\n**Approach**\n\nIn a tetrahedral field the d-orbital splitting is inverted relative to octahedral. Because of this, tetrahedral d⁷ is treated with the same energy matrix as octahedral d³. The ground term is ⁴A₂(F), and the three spin-allowed bands are:\n\n- ν₁: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F), with energy ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F)\n- ν₃: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P)\n\nThe energies of ν₂ and ν₃ are:\n\nν₂,₃ = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½·√(225B² − 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²)\n\nso ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n\n**Solution**\n\n1. From ν₁: 10Dq = 4 330 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 433 cm⁻¹** (Δt = 4 330 cm⁻¹).\n2. From the sum of the upper two bands: ν₂ + ν₃ = 26 160 = 30(433) + 15B = 12 990 + 15B, so **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Check**\n\nPut Dq = 433 cm⁻¹ and B = 878 cm⁻¹ back into the formula:\n\n- The square-root term is √(1.2377×10⁸) ≈ 11 125 cm⁻¹.\n- ν₂ = 6 495 + 6 585 − 5 563 ≈ **7 518 cm⁻¹** (observed 7 520).\n- ν₃ ≈ **18 643 cm⁻¹** (observed 18 640).\n\nAll three bands are reproduced within about 3 cm⁻¹, so the band assignment holds together.\n\n**Covalency**\n\nThe free Co²⁺ ion has B ≈ 1 120 cm⁻¹. That gives a nephelauxetic ratio β ≈ 0.78, meaning the metal–ligand bonds are moderately covalent.\n\n**Racah C**\n\nC can't be determined from these bands. All three are quartet → quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B, not on C.\n\n{\"Dq\": 433, \"B\": 878, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Td d7 treated as Oh d3 analogue: 10Dq = ν1, B from ν2+ν3 = 30Dq+15B; C not determinable from quartet bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7974,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"eph